/// Video gateway (issue 158): the cameras and their frames. `init` gives the gateway's only client to the shell, which
/// lends it in SLOT_CAMERA to a program that asks for REQUEST_CAMERA once the user agreed (MC-11.4). A camera has one
/// owner at a time, and each camera its own stream, so two programs show two cameras at once (158-DRV-0082); while any
/// stream is open the compositor shows a camera mark that no program can hide. Pixels are 0x00RRGGBB, row after row, as
/// on a screen. Without a camera the gateway lists none, unless the boot disk holds `video/synthetic`: then a moving
/// test pattern stands in (the tests' source, as the WAV files are for voice), or two when the file begins with `2`.
///
/// 1.1 (158-DRV-0083): `modes` lists a camera's modes, each a frame size it sends in an encoding at one of its rates,
/// and says which the gateway offers; `open` asked for an offered mode's size and rate streams that mode unscaled, and
/// `opened` says which mode the camera sends. The calls of 1.0 are unchanged on the wire, so a 1.0 program works with a
/// 1.1 gateway; a 1.0 gateway refuses `modes` and `opened` (the call is rejected as invalid).
///
/// 1.2 (158-DRV-0084): the gateway decodes Motion JPEG, so a camera's MJPEG modes within the sizes and rates it takes
/// are offered too, and `open` may stream one. Nothing changes on the wire: a 1.1 program works with a 1.2 gateway.
package mind:video@1.2.0;

interface video {
    enum error { not-found, busy, invalid, not-open, no-memory }
    /// A camera: its name, the largest picture and rate it gives, and whether it is the synthetic test source.
    record camera { name: string<24>, width: u16, height: u16, rate: u8, synthetic: bool }
    /// A frame: its number since the stream opened (from 1), when it was taken (monotonic clock, µs), its size and the
    /// bytes copied into the lent buffer.
    record frame { sequence: u32, timestamp-us: u64, width: u16, height: u16, bytes: u32 }
    /// 1.1: how a mode's frames come from the camera: YUY2 (which the gateway converts), Motion JPEG (which it decodes,
    /// 1.2), another uncompressed format, or drawn by the gateway (the test pattern).
    enum encoding { yuy2, mjpeg, uncompressed, synthetic }
    /// 1.1: a mode: a frame size in an encoding at one of the camera's frame intervals (100 ns units, as the camera
    /// states it), so at `rate` frames a second (10^7 / interval rounded down; 0 for less than one a second); `offered`
    /// when the gateway streams it now: a mode it decodes (YUY2; Motion JPEG too, 1.2) within the sizes and rates it
    /// takes, which the camera's streaming settings carry.
    record mode { encoding: encoding, width: u16, height: u16, rate: u8, interval: u32, offered: bool }
    /// 1.1: the caller's stream: the camera's number when it was opened, the mode the camera sends (its place in that
    /// camera's `modes`, 65535 for a test pattern drawn at a size none of its modes has, and its facts), and whether
    /// its frames are scaled to another size, the one asked for.
    record opened { camera: u8, index: u16, mode: mode, scaled: bool }

    /// The cameras, in the order `open` numbers them.
    cameras: func() -> list<camera, 4>;
    /// Opens camera `camera` at `width` × `height` and `rate` frames a second and makes the caller its owner; busy
    /// while another task owns it (a stream whose owner ended can be taken over); invalid for a size or rate it lacks.
    /// A caller's earlier stream, of this camera or another, is closed first. 1.1: asked for an offered mode's size and
    /// rate, the camera streams that mode and its frames come unscaled; another size is the nearest mode's, scaled. A
    /// listed mode that does not open says why: busy, another task owns the camera; not-found, the camera is gone (or
    /// the number is past the list); invalid, the gateway does not offer that size or rate now.
    open: func(camera: u8, width: u16, height: u16, rate: u8) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The next frame of the caller's stream into the lent buffer (at most `capacity` bytes); waits until it is due.
    /// Returns its sequence number.
    read: func(capacity: u32, pixels: borrow<memory>) -> result<u32, error>;
    /// The frame `read` copied last.
    frame: func() -> result<frame, error>;
    /// Closes the caller's stream.
    close: func() -> result<_, error>;
    /// 1.1: camera `camera`'s modes, at most 256, in the order its descriptors give them: each frame size of each format
    /// at each of its intervals (of a continuous range, its shortest and its longest). Not-found for a camera past the list.
    modes: func(camera: u8) -> result<list<mode, 256>, error>;
    /// 1.1: the caller's stream as `open` set it up; not-open without one.
    opened: func() -> result<opened, error>;
}
